/target
Cargo.lock.bak
**/*.rs.bk
*.swp
.DS_Store

# Test/playwright ephemeral artifacts
.meta-snapshots/
.playwright-mcp/

# Agent harness output (generated, not committed)
.agent/
.archive/

# envctl.lock IS committed — it is the content-hashed manifest-of-record and the
# baseline for `envctl lock --check` (the CI drift gate). Do not ignore it.

# .handoff is git-tracked TEXT in full — the complete continuity audit trail (backlog.md /
# loop_state.md / HANDOFF.md / cycle/ / _done/ deliveries/ AND the loop control sentinels
# DONE/NEEDS-HUMAN/STOP and run logs) is committed so a fresh session resumes cold with nothing
# left behind.
#
# ADR-0018 D1 fail-safe (the ONE binary exception, CI-enforced by ci/gates/p7.sh §3c):
# `.handoff/ledger.events.jsonl` is the committed continuity truth; `.handoff/ledger.db` and
# sidecars are local redb/RVF rebuild caches derived from that JSONL. Never commit binary ledgers.
.handoff/**/ledger.db
.handoff/**/ledger.db.*

# Runtime claim locks are ephemeral coordination state, not durable handoff text.
.handoff/locks/

# GitKB — track the durable TEXT store (documents/commits/refs/manifest.json =
# the source of truth) so the KB survives clone/reclaim and travels in git.
# Ignore ONLY the rebuildable index cache (`git-kb reindex` regenerates .cache
# from store) and the ephemeral editing surfaces. Mirrors the `.handoff` rule
# above (track text; never commit binary rebuild caches).
#   NOTE: `git-kb init`'s tool default ignores `.kb/store/` wholesale — that is
#   WRONG here: it makes the KB non-durable (nothing committed/pushed; the docs
#   are lost on a fresh clone). See META-ORG-POLICY (.kb store-durability rule).
.kb/.cache/
.kb/workspace/
.kb/workspaces/
.kb/store/stashes/
